/*****************************************************************************
    retProbe -- WHAT DOES AN INTERPRETED ACTION RETURN? 2026-07-31.

    THE BONES ITEM behind the return-value ruling: the compiled action must
    return what the INTERPRETED action returns -- one semantics, not two -- so
    the emitter has to mirror a rule nobody had written down. This file reads it
    off the source AND runs it, because a source read grades `inferred` in this
    project and only a run grades `verified`.

    THE CHAIN, cited so it can be re-checked rather than re-trusted:

      runAction        GroupActions.rtn:572   result = processAction(field);
                                       :574   return result;
      processAction    GroupActions.rtn:447   if result = action["BlocK"] {
                                       :457     if result = result.gMethod(result)
                                                    result.isBranch = 0; }
                                       :460   return result;
      aCTionBlocK      ruleActions.rtn:21-26  while grup = input.next(grup) {
                                                  if grup.isMethod result = grup.gMethod(grup);
                                                  else             result = grup;
                                                  if result && isBranch break; }
                                              if result && isGROUP result = gGroup;
                                              return result;

    SO THE RULE, read off that chain:
      1. An action's value is THE VALUE OF THE LAST STATEMENT EXECUTED. There is
         no implicit null, no implicit argument, no falseResult default.
      2. `return`/`break`/`continue` set isBranch, which BREAKS the statement
         loop early -- so an explicit return is just "stop here", and the value
         is still the last statement's value.
      3. A group-valued result is DEREFERENCED (`result = gGroup`) on the way
         out of the block.
      4. isBranch is CLEARED at the action boundary (processAction:457), so a
         return's branch flag does not leak into the caller's statement loop.
      5. A BARE return yields the PRIOR statement's value (ratified 2026-07-31).
         Rules 1 and 5 are the same rule: a branch statement that carries no
         value cannot BE the value, so the last valued statement stands.

    WHY THAT MATTERS FOR THE EMITTER: under the result-slot mechanism, EVERY
    statement's value would have to reach the slot, not just the ones written
    `return` -- because rule 1 says the last statement wins whether or not it
    said so. That is a materially different emitter from "store on return".

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT (standing harness rule H2).
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;

register(RetProbe);
define
    zqv;

    /*  last statement is an ASSIGNMENT -- rule 1 says its value is the result */
    tailAssign code={ zqv = 41; };

    /*  last statement is a PRINT -- what does a print statement evaluate to?  */
    tailPrint code={ zqv = 42; print "    (tailPrint printed)":; };

    /*  explicit return of a value -- the control                              */
    tailExplicit code={ zqv = 43; return zqv; };

    /*  BARE return -- RATIFIED BY TONY 2026-07-31: bare return means STOP, and
        the action's value is the LAST EXECUTED STATEMENT'S. So this yields 44,
        the assignment's value.
        It used to yield the STRING "return": aCTionBrancH falls back to the
        BrancheS keyword node when there is no expression, and a node with no
        data returns its own TAG (CLAIM KANT-10). A leak, never a semantics.
        Fixed in aCTionBlocK -- see its header for why it could not live in
        aCTionBrancH alone.                                                    */
    tailBare code={ zqv = 44; return; };

    /*  an early return with statements AFTER it: proves isBranch really does
        break the loop rather than merely marking the value                     */
    tailEarly code={ zqv = 45; return zqv; zqv = 999; };
    ;

print "1 tail is an assignment  ->[" tailAssign() "]  (want 41 if last-statement-wins)":;
print "2 tail is a print        ->[" tailPrint() "]":;
print "3 explicit return value  ->[" tailExplicit() "]  (want 43)":;
print "4 bare return            ->[" tailBare() "]  (want 44 = prior statement)":;
print "5 early return, code after ->[" tailEarly() "]  (want 45; 999 = the break failed)":;
print "  zqv after row 5        ->[" zqv "]  (want 45; 999 = statements after return RAN)":;

print "RP SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
